      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I've solved the issue for myself with 100% reproducibility. If this solves it for anyone else, let me know.\n\nIn Windows' \"Virus & threat protections settings\" you must enable \"Real-time protection.\" Wait 30 seconds (or reboot) and try launch the game again. That's all.\n\nWith this setting off, I can't launch VRChat under any circumstance. This was a big headache to solve, so this is also on a completely fresh install of Windows. \n\nSome further details: I've tried all other solutions listed here with no success. The memory integrity setting also has no effect for me.\n\nI'm fairly sure this is related to some conflict with EAC: launching the game via VRChat.exe will always succeed. Using launch.exe (with EAC) did not.\n\nI tried disassembling the crash dump and debugging, and came to a few conclusions that another user also mentioned:\n\nThe crash routine was matched to BDWGC GC_suspend() in win32_threads.c. The failing branch is repeated failure of GetThreadContext() after a successful SuspendThread(). The identical routine was live-debugged with VRChat.exe, where GetThreadContext() consistently returned TRUE (EAX=1, Win32 LastError 0). In the normal EAC launch, it exhausts MAX_SUSPEND_THREAD_RETRIES (1,000,000) and executes the fatal int 3. \n\nTo a developer: if you could instrument/log GetLastError() immediately after failed GetThreadContext(), the target TID/handle, and the handle’s granted access mask under the protected launch, that might yield some answers.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
